schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Typing vs Tagging"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Two distinct classification jobs that get confused when treated as one:
    typing answers "what kind of thing is this?" (a small, stable set —
    guide, tool, case study, meeting note); tagging answers "what is this
    about, and how might I want to filter it?" (a large, growing set of
    topics, domains, audiences).
  source_traditions:
    - library/information science cataloguing (type vs subject heading)
    - the Regen Toolkit's own ontology layer
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Framed in the article as the reason AI classification projects go wrong:
    asking a model one mushy question ("classify this") instead of two clear
    ones (what kind, what about). The article quotes the Toolkit's ontology
    layer directly: "A type defines what something is. A tag describes how
    something may be classified, interpreted, filtered, or used. Confusing
    tags with types creates ontology sprawl."
  adjacent_meanings:
    - categorization (used loosely to mean either typing or tagging)
    - taxonomy (the overall system of types + tags together)
  important_distinctions:
    - "type set is small and stable; tag set is large and grows over time"
    - "keeping type and tag in separate schema fields makes the AI's job easier because each question has one clear answer"
  tensions:
    - >-
      teams often reach for one all-purpose "category" field, which the
      article argues quietly recreates the sprawl problem it was meant to
      solve.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating "classification" as a single undifferentiated task erases the
    distinction the article treats as load-bearing — a taxonomy that mixes
    types and tags in one field cannot be constrained to a stable enum,
    because the tag half is supposed to keep growing.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ai-assisted-classification.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-e50c8f7e3b5d
  source_lineage: ai-assisted-classification.md
